    "updated_at": "2019-10-25T21:39:00Z",
    "body": "> On second look, I don't see a contradiction either. The spec doesn't say that the tokenizer must wait for the tree constructor to finish its steps. That's just how I imaged it looking in code. Thanks for the clarification.\r\n\r\nI think logically it must, though? When the tree constructor can mutate the tokenizer state it clearly can't operate entirely in parallel. You'd have to know when you can safety resume the tokenizer.\r\n\r\nThis seems like a bug introduced from when we started skipping LF in `textarea` and `pre` and while it's obvious what the intent of the spec is I'm not sure how we can resolve this easily. I think the easiest thing might be getting rid of \"next token\" entirely and adding a new insertion mode that skips an LF character then switches to text?",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
